.TH TMD 1 "September 17, 2026" "tmd 1.1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmd \- dump the metadata out of a tar archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmd
.BI \-f " FILE"
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B tmd
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B tmd
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmd
reads a tar archive and reports what is inside it without extracting anything.
By default it prints one
.BR ls (1)\-style
line per member: the permission bits, the owner and group, the size, the
modification time the archive recorded, and the path.
.PP
The archive is opened for reading and nothing else. No member is written to
disk, no data is decoded, and the file is never modified.
.PP
Three families of tar archive are understood:
.TP
.B BSD
\fBustar\fR (POSIX.1\-1988) and \fBpax\fR (POSIX.1\-2001) archives, as
.BR bsdtar (1)
and libarchive write them, including pax extended headers for long paths,
high\-numbered user IDs, and sub\-second timestamps.
.TP
.B GNU
.BR tar (1)
archives, including the
.B L
and
.B K
blocks that carry long names and link targets, the old sparse\-file format with
its in\-header map and continuation blocks, and the GNU.sparse.* pax formats
0.0, 0.1 and 1.0.
.TP
.B v7
Pre\-POSIX archives with no magic, no user names and no path prefix, as the
original Seventh Edition
.BR tar (1)
wrote them.
.PP
The format is worked out per member rather than per archive, because a real
archive mixes them: a pax archive is plain ustar headers with an occasional
extended header in front of the member that needed one. The summary reports the
most expressive dialect present, because that is the one a reader has to
understand to get every member right.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-file=FILE
archive to read; repeatable. Without it, standard input is read
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output=FILE
write the report to FILE instead of standard output
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-long
one block per member with every field, rather than one line
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-raw\-headers
also show the raw 512\-byte header fields (implies \-\-long)
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-info
report what the archive IS: format generation, extensions, integrity
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-summary
print only the archive summary, no member listing
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-with\-summary
print the listing and then the summary
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-numeric\-owner
show numeric uid/gid instead of the names stored in the archive
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-human
print sizes as 1.4K and 23M rather than in bytes
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-local
render timestamps in this machine's zone instead of UTC
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-utc
render timestamps as UTC (the default; accepted for scripts)
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-full\-time
include seconds, nanoseconds and the zone offset in timestamps
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-check
check the archive for damage (checksums, truncation); exit 3 on any
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
do not write warnings about damaged headers to standard error
.TP
.B     \-\-format=FMT
output format: text (the default), json or csv
.TP
.B     \-\-color[=WHEN]
colorize the listing: auto (the default), always or never
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
show this help and exit
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
show the version and exit
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
the archive was read
.TP
.B 1
the archive could not be read
.TP
.B 2
the command line was wrong
.TP
.B 3
\-\-check found damage: a bad checksum or a truncated archive
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B tmd \-f archive.tar
list every member, ls \-l style
.TP
.B tmd \-f archive.tar \-l
every field of every member
.TP
.B tmd \-f archive.tar \-i
what the archive is: format, extensions, integrity
.TP
.B tmd \-f archive.tar \-s
just the summary: format, counts, sizes
.TP
.B tmd \-f archive.tar \-o report.txt
write the report to a file
.TP
.B tmd \-f archive.tar \-\-format=json
machine\-readable output for a script
.TP
.B gzip \-dc a.tar.gz | tmd
read a compressed archive through a pipe
